Organizations and developers use several defensive strategies to protect applications from vulnerabilities. Security teams categorize these protections into four distinct layers: Fixing (Code), Shielding (Network), Hardening (System), and Monitoring (Detection). [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
Here is every method used to defend an application against existing security flaws:
## 1. Code-Level Fixes (The Gold Standard)
Fixing the source code directly removes the structural flaw, making the vulnerability impossible to trigger. [6] 

* Parameterized Queries: Uses prepared statements to separate executable code from user data, preventing SQL and command injections.
* Strict Whitelisting: Rejects any input that does not match a rigid pattern (e.g., forcing a username to contain only numbers using ^[0-9]+$).
* Input Type Casting: Explicitly converts string inputs into safe data types (e.g., forcing a value to become a strict integer or boolean) before processing.
* Safe API Alternatives: Replaces dangerous system functions like eval() or exec() with native language features or safe library modules. [7, 8, 9, 10, 11] 

## 2. Network and Virtual Shielding (The Perimeter)
When code cannot be changed immediately, organizations use network devices to intercept and drop malicious traffic.

* Web Application Firewalls (WAF): Scans incoming HTTP requests for known attack signatures, blocking patterns like sleep 10 or ; netcat.
* Virtual Patching: Deploying a temporary rule to an API gateway or proxy to drop specific exploit strings while developers write a permanent fix.
* Rate Limiting & Throttling: Restricts the number of requests an IP can make per second to stop automated brute-force or fuzzing tools.
* Content Delivery Network (CDN) Filtering: Drops malicious payloads at the edge network before they ever reach the origin server. [12, 13, 14, 15, 16] 

## 3. System and OS Hardening (Damage Control)
If an attacker successfully bypasses the firewall and exploits the code, hardening prevents them from taking over the server. [17, 18] 

* Principle of Least Privilege: Runs the web server process under an isolated user account (like www-data) that lacks permission to run administrative utilities. [19, 20, 21] 
* Container Isolation (Docker/Kubernetes): Confines the application inside a temporary container sandbox, stopping an attacker from accessing the host operating system. [22, 23, 24, 25] 
* Read-Only File Systems: Configures the application directory so it cannot be modified, blocking attackers from writing malicious backdoors or reverse shell scripts. [26, 27, 28] 
* Network Segmentation: Places the web server in an isolated zone (DMZ) so an attacker cannot pivot to internal databases or company computers. [29, 30, 31, 32] 

## 4. Continuous Monitoring and Detection (The Safety Net)
Monitoring detects active exploitation attempts and alerts security teams to respond immediately. [33, 34] 

* Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR): Monitors server operating systems for anomalous processes, such as a web server process (node, php, python) suddenly launching a system shell (/bin/sh or cmd.exe). [35, 36, 37, 38, 39] 
* SIEM Alert Rules: Aggregates web server logs to look for high concentrations of evasion characters (like carets ^ or backslashes \) inside URL parameters.
* Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS): Analyzes network packet behavior to spot reverse shells or out-of-band data exfiltration connections. [40, 41, 42] 

